  OptBestText = the 2.6\,GHz clock cap cuts a run by 8.7\,\% on IRIS and one process per L3 cache with the 2.0\,GHz cap cuts a run by 20.0\,\% on Aion
  OptCampaignGramsAll = 1\,100
  OptCampaignText = 120 runs on IRIS and 158 runs on Aion (plus 13 whose results were not used, in allocations stopped early)
  OptCapText = At 2.0\,GHz on Aion, the power changed by $-$16.7\,\% and the run time by +3.4\,\%, so the energy changed by $-$13.9\,\%, the operational emissions by $-$13.9\,\%, the manufacture charged to the run by +3.4\,\%, and the footprint by $-$3.9\,\% (lower in every round). At 1.5\,GHz on Aion, 5 of the 10 runs did 150 iterations instead of 200 and are left out. In the 5 that did the same work, the power changed by $-$19.6\,\% and the run time by +9.7\,\%, so the energy changed by $-$11.8\,\%, the operational emissions by $-$11.8\,\%, the manufacture charged to the run by +9.7\,\%, and the footprint by +0.6\,\% (higher in every round).
  OptFlagsPctText = Going back from \texttt{-O3} to \texttt{-O2} changed the footprint of a run by +0.1\,\% on Aion; adding \texttt{-march=native} changed it by $-$0.1\,\% on Aion.
  OptFlagsText = On Aion the two levels cannot be told apart from the variation between rounds, and \texttt{-march=native} made no difference beyond that variation.
  OptLeftOutNote =  On IRIS, the first of the 10 rounds is left out (Section~\ref{sec:afteropt}).
  OptPlacementText = Giving each process its own L3 cache changed the rating by +24.0\,\%, the run time by $-$18.2\,\% and the power by +4.2\,\%, so the footprint changed by $-$16.7\,\% (lower in every round) and the footprint per unit of work by $-$16.7\,\%. With the 2.0\,GHz cap as well, the footprint changed by $-$21.7\,\% (lower in every round). The 2.0\,GHz cap alone changed it by $-$4.7\,\% (lower in every round) in this allocation, against $-$3.9\,\% in the first.
  OptResultText = On IRIS, the change kept is the 2.6\,GHz clock cap. Its 100 runs emitted 5.32\,g per run (standard deviation 0.02\,g), against 5.82\,g for the 9 runs of the unchanged job on the same node, a change of $-$8.7\,\%. A run took 216.8\,s instead of 214.1\,s (+1.3\,\%), used 47.7 instead of 58.8\,kJ ($-$18.9\,\%) and rated 12.4\,GFLOP/s, the same to one decimal ($-$0.5\,\%); HPCG validated all of them, each with 100 iterations. The first of the 10 rounds on this node is left out of that comparison: its unchanged run took 245\,s and rated 10.9\,GFLOP/s, against 213 to 215\,s and 12.4 to 12.5\,GFLOP/s for the other 9, and the capped run of that round was slow too (225\,s against 217 to 220\,s); its clock and CPU use were those of the other runs, and nothing else the job recorded explains it. With that round, the change would be $-$9.8\,\%. In the rounds on this node, it emitted less than the unchanged job in 9 of 9 rounds, by 8.4\,\% on average. On another node, the 100 runs of Section~\ref{sec:lcia} emitted 5.98\,g, and this node's unchanged runs 2.5\,\% less: a difference between IRIS's nodes, which is why the comparison rests on the same node. On Aion, the change kept is one process per L3 cache with the 2.0\,GHz cap. Its 100 runs emitted 2.51\,g per run (standard deviation 0.009\,g), against 3.13\,g for the 100 runs of the unchanged job in Section~\ref{sec:lcia}, a change of $-$20.0\,\%. A run took 111.0\,s instead of 126.5\,s ($-$12.2\,\%), used 27.8 instead of 40.1\,kJ ($-$30.6\,\%) and rated 35.4 instead of 30.5\,GFLOP/s (+16.3\,\%); HPCG validated all of them, each with 200 iterations. In the test rounds on one node, it emitted less than the unchanged job in 2 of 2 rounds, by 21.7\,\% on average.
  OptSaveText = 0.51\,g on IRIS and 0.63\,g on Aion
  OptValidityText = On IRIS, HPCG validated all 20 runs of its rounds, all with the same scaled residual. Every configuration did the baseline's 100 iterations. On Aion (flags and clock), HPCG validated all 50 screening runs, all with the same scaled residual. Every configuration did at least the baseline's 200 iterations except the 1.5\,GHz cap, which did 150 in 5 of its 10 runs. HPCG repeats its set of 50 iterations as many times as fit in the 60\,s that \texttt{hpcg.dat} requests, plus one, so a configuration that runs a set slightly slower can do one set fewer, which the rule of Section~\ref{sec:optproc} excludes, and a faster one a set more. On Aion (placement), HPCG validated all 8 screening runs, all with the same scaled residual. Every configuration did the baseline's 200 iterations.
  PaybackCostHi = 1\,500
  PaybackCostLo = 1\,100
  PaybackMeaningText = The optimisation cost 1\,100 to 1\,500\,g. At the savings measured here, it would take about 1\,819 to 3\,010 comparable runs to recover that cost.
  PaybackText = On IRIS the estimated payback is 2\,246 to 3\,010 runs. The 7 matching allocations on IRIS would imply approximately 321 to 430 years under this assumption. On Aion the estimated payback is 1\,819 to 2\,438 runs. The 3 matching allocations on Aion would imply approximately 606 to 813 years under this assumption.
  PerSecondRatio = 1.1
  RatingRatio = 2.5
